"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640896.5491648\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640896.5491648\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"Test2\",\"final_eval_status\":2,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.59251968503937,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-92e9106e-165a-4126-94ef-6c48c0bff47e\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: # 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion in Kyoto, blending iconic temples, traditional arts, and natural serenity, structured logically for efficient travel using Kyoto's bus and train network.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Pre-Trip Essentials & Budget Estimates\\n\\n**Best Travel Season Recommendation:** Late October or Mid-November (Autumn Foliage).\\n**Transportation Tip:** Purchase an **ICOCA Card** upon arrival for seamless travel on buses and trains. Budget for a **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass** (approx. 700 JPY) on heavy bus travel days (Days 2, 3).\\n\\n| Major Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated 7-Day Total (JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-Range) | 15,000 - 25,000 | 105,000 - 175,000 | *Ryokan* stay recommended for 1-2 nights. |\\n| **Food & Dining** | 5,000 - 10,000 | 35,000 - 70,000 | Includes street food, casual lunches, and 1-2 higher-end dinners. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | 1,500 - 2,500 | 10,500 - 17,500 | Average temple entry is 500-600 JPY. |\\n| **Local Transport** | 1,000 - 1,500 | 7,000 - 10,500 | IC card use + occasional day passes. |\\n| **Cultural Activities** (Workshops/Tea) | Variable (Budget 5,000 JPY per activity) | ~15,000 | Allocated for 2 major workshops/ceremonies. |\\n| **Estimated Total (Excluding Flights/Insurance)** | **23,000 - 39,000+** | **Approx. 172,500 - 283,000 JPY** | *(USD conversion highly recommended for budgeting)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying near the historical centers offers convenience and atmosphere:\\n\\n1.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, proximity to traditional streets, and evening walks. Excellent access to many major eastern temples.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Best for modern convenience, dining variety, and easy subway/bus connectivity to all directions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Gion Immersion (Tradition Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Dining Recommendation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Arrival & Check-in.** Activate ICOCA card. | 2 hours | **Lunch:** Casual **Udon/Soba** near your accommodation. |\\n| **3:00 PM** | **Tea Ceremony Experience.** Book a private or small-group ceremony in the Gion or Higashiyama area to begin your cultural immersion immediately. (Pre-book essential!) | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Explore Hanamikoji Street (Gion).** Walk the historic streets, observing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. | 1 hour | |\\n| **6:30 PM** | **Dinner & Geiko Spotting.** Dine early. Walk cautiously through Gion after dark, respecting the privacy of *Geiko* and *Maiko*. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Try **Kyoto-style *Obanzai*** (home-style cooking) in a small Gion eatery. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to accommodation. | - | |\\n\\n**Cultural Note:** Pay close attention to the etiquette learned in the tea ceremony (removing shoes, sitting posture).\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty (Iconic Temples & Preserved Streets)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:30 AM** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple.** Arrive *before* 8:00 AM to enjoy the wooden stage with minimal crowds. | 2 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:00 AM** | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka.** Wander down these iconic, preserved sloping streets. Browse traditional craft shops. | 1.5 hours | **Snack:** Try *Yatsuhashi* (cinnamon sweet). |\\n| **11:30 AM** | **Kodaiji Temple.** A beautiful Zen temple nearby, often quieter than Kiyomizu-dera. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Higashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Vegetarian *Sh\u014djin Ry\u014dri* (Zen Buddhist Cuisine) if available nearby, or a light cafe lunch. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Yasaka Shrine & Maruyama Park.** Explore the main shrine and relax in the adjacent park. | 1.5 hours | Walkable from the previous stop. |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Optional: Visit Chion-in Temple** (Impressive main gate). | 1 hour | |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner & Relaxation.** | | **Dinner:** Enjoy **Ramen** in the Kawaramachi area for a hearty meal. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Grandeur and Zen Rock Gardens (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:30 AM** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion).** Aim for opening time to see the best reflections before peak crowds arrive. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Ryoan-ji Temple.** Famous for its abstract, enigmatic rock garden\u2014a pinnacle of Zen aesthetics. | 1.5 hours | Short bus ride or 25 min walk from Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Temples.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Simple set meal (*teishoku*) at a local eatery. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Ninna-ji Temple (If time allows).** A large complex with beautiful pagodas and temple grounds. | 2 hours | |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Return to City Center.** Browse for high-quality crafts or traditional items. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **Evening** | **Special Dinner Experience.** | | **Dinner:** Splurge on **Kyoto Beef** or enjoy high-quality **Sushi**. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates and Sacred Statues (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:00 AM** | **Fushimi Inari Shrine.** Start *very early* (before 7:30 AM) to experience the magic of the *torii* gates without the crush of tourists. Hike as far as desired. | 3 - 4 hours | **Transport:** JR Nara Line (ICOCA Card) to Inari Station. |\\n| **11:00 AM** | **Return & Travel North.** Head toward Higashiyama/Central area. | 1 hour | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near Sanjusangendo.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Quick, local stop near the temple. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Sanjusangendo Temple.** Marvel at the 1,001 statues of Kannon. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access is best from Kyoto Station area. |\\n| **4:00 PM** | **Kyoto National Museum (Optional).** Deepen your understanding of Japanese art and history. | 2 hours | Located close to Sanjusangendo. |\\n| **Evening** | **Relaxed Dinner.** | | **Dinner:** Explore **Pontocho Alley** for atmosphere and dining options along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature and Bamboo Serenity (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:00 AM** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** Head here immediately upon arrival in the area to walk through the grove before the major crowds arrive. | 1 hour | **Transport:** JR Sagano Line (ICOCA) to Saga-Arashiyama Station. |\\n| **9:30 AM** | **Tenryu-ji Temple.** Explore the stunning Zen garden, which transitions beautifully into the surrounding natural landscape. | 2 hours | Adjacent to the Bamboo Grove exit. |\\n| **12:00 PM** | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk.** Enjoy views of the Katsura River. | 1 hour | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Arashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Okochi Sanso Villa.** The former estate of a famous actor. Offers incredible panoramic views and a quieter experience than the main grove. Includes tea upon exit. | 2 hours | A slight climb, but highly rewarding. |\\n| **Evening** | **Return to Central Kyoto.** | | **Dinner:** Casual dinner near your hotel or try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n\\n### Day 6: Philosophers' Path and Cultural Workshop (Northeast Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion).** Appreciate its dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku No Michi).** Walk south along the picturesque canal, stopping to admire smaller shrines. | 1.5 - 2 hours | Perfect for quiet reflection. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Path.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Cafe lunch or quick bite near the northern end of the path. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Specialized Cultural Workshop.** Book a workshop for deeper engagement (e.g., *Ikebana* flower arranging, calligraphy, or *Wagashi* sweet making). | 2.5 hours | Confirm location beforehand; often near central or eastern Kyoto. |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Optional: Nanzen-ji Temple.** Explore the grounds and the striking aqueduct near the entrance. | 1.5 hours | Beautiful spot as the sun starts to start to set. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner Planning.** | | **Dinner:** Final meal emphasizing your favorite Japanese cuisine from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Quiet Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Honen-in Temple OR Daitoku-ji Complex.** Choose a less crowded, more reflective temple site to conclude your temple visits. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access for both locations. |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Souvenir Shopping.** Pick up last-minute quality gifts (high-grade green tea, incense, crafts). Nishiki Market is an option, but can be crowded. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Final Lunch.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** A final, favorite Japanese meal or something quick near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Departure.** Travel to Kyoto Station for Shinkansen or airport access. | As required | Allow ample time for airport transfers. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Etiquette Reminder:** Remember to practice respectful silence in temples, thank staff with *Okini*, and never photograph *Geiko* without permission. Enjoy your profound cultural journey!\\n\\n---\\n\\n## OPTIMIZATION TIPS: Money-Saving & Practical Advice\\n\\n### Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Embrace Convenience Store Food:** Japanese convenience stores (*Konbini* like 7-Eleven, FamilyMart, Lawson) offer high-quality, fresh, and extremely cheap meals (onigiri, sandwiches, bento boxes). Use these for quick breakfasts or picnic lunches.\\n2.  **Maximize Bus Passes:** If you visit 3 or more major bus-accessible sites in one day (especially Days 2, 3, 6), the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (700 JPY)** pays for itself over the standard two-ride fare (230 JPY x 3 = 690 JPY).\\n3.  **Free Temple Sightseeing:** Many major temples (like Fushimi Inari and Yasaka Shrine) are free. Focus your spending on 1-2 high-priority temples per day and enjoy the grounds of others.\\n4.  **Lunch is the Key Meal:** Many high-end restaurants offer fantastic **lunch sets (*teishoku*)** for half the price of their dinner services. If you want to try Kyoto Beef or high-end sushi, do it at lunch.\\n5.  **Water Refills:** Carry a reusable water bottle. Many public parks, JR stations, and some temples/museums have free, safe drinking water refill stations. Avoid buying bottled water constantly.\\n\\n### Local Apps, Websites, and Resources\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for navigating Kyoto's complex bus system. It provides real-time departure/arrival estimates for all public transport.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (or Navitime for Japan Travel):** Excellent for checking train/subway routes, especially for longer trips (e.g., to Arashiyama).\\n3.  **Google Translate (Download Offline Japanese Pack):** Invaluable for reading smaller signs, menus, and asking specific questions.\\n4.  **Official Kyoto Tourism Website:** Check for any temporary closures or specific cultural events happening during your travel dates.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: Packing & Preparation\\n\\n### Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\n1.  **Comfortable, Slip-On Shoes:** You will remove your shoes constantly (temples, tea houses, some restaurants). Wear shoes that are easy to kick off and put back on, and ensure your socks are clean and hole-free!\\n2.  **Layers (Especially for Autumn/Spring):** Weather fluctuates significantly between sunny afternoons and chilly evenings. Bring light layers, a versatile scarf, and a light, water-resistant jacket.\\n3.  **Small Crossbody Bag/Backpack:** Essential for carrying water, guidebooks, and purchases, while leaving your hands free for bowing, photography, and paying for fares.\\n4.  **Portable Wi-Fi Hotspot or SIM Card:** Essential for navigation and real-time transport lookups, as public Wi-Fi is unreliable outside major hubs.\\n5.  **Small Towel/Handkerchief:** Public restrooms often lack paper towels or air dryers.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## BACKUP PLANS & Contingency Strategies\\n\\n### Weather Contingencies (Rainy Days)\\n\\nKyoto has excellent indoor cultural options that work perfectly if rain strikes on Days 2, 3, or 6:\\n\\n*   **If Rain Hits Higashiyama (Day 2):** Substitute outdoor time with extended visits to the **Kyoto National Museum** (already scheduled as optional on Day 4, but can be swapped in) or visit the **International Manga Museum** (central Kyoto).\\n*   **If Rain Hits Northern Kyoto (Day 3):** Focus heavily on the **Kyoto Imperial Palace** grounds (some buildings are covered) or spend more time at **Ninna-ji Temple**, which has extensive covered corridors.\\n*   **Workshop Focus:** If the weather is bad, dedicate Day 6's afternoon workshop to a longer session (e.g., a full-day pottery class instead of just a 2-hour workshop) to maximize indoor time.\\n\\n### Crowds/Unexpected Closures\\n\\n*   **Fushimi Inari (Day 4):** If the main path is too crowded at 7:00 AM, hike higher up the mountain. The crowds thin out dramatically the further you walk past the main viewing areas.\\n*   **Temple Overload Backup:** If you feel temple fatigue, substitute a planned temple visit with a trip to **Nishiki Market** (Kyoto's Kitchen) on Day 7 or earlier. It\u2019s a vibrant sensory experience focused on food culture rather than history.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## CULTURAL DO'S AND DON'TS\\n\\n| Do's (Respectful Travel) | Don'ts (Cultural Missteps to Avoid) |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** use *Arigato Gozaimasu* (Thank you). Learn *Okini* for Kyoto-specific gratitude. | **DON'T** eat or drink while walking down the street, especially near shrines or on public transport. |\\n| **DO** carry a small, sealable plastic bag for your own trash; public bins are scarce. | **DON'T** walk or stand on the threshold of a traditional doorway (*tobira*). |\\n| **DO** bow slightly when thanking service staff or entering/exiting small shops. | **DON'T** tip staff; it can be confusing or taken as an insult. |\\n| **DO** wait patiently in designated queues for all transport and attractions. | **DON'T** try to hail taxis aggressively; they stop when they are free and available. |\\n| **DO** follow purification rituals (*temizu*) correctly at shrines. | **DON'T** block the path of *Maiko* or *Geiko* in Gion for photos; view them respectfully from a distance. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640875.600524,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: # 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion in Kyoto, blending iconic temples, traditional arts, and natural serenity, structured logically for efficient travel using Kyoto's bus and train network.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Pre-Trip Essentials & Budget Estimates\\n\\n**Best Travel Season Recommendation:** Late October or Mid-November (Autumn Foliage).\\n**Transportation Tip:** Purchase an **ICOCA Card** upon arrival for seamless travel on buses and trains. Budget for a **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass** (approx. 700 JPY) on heavy bus travel days (Days 2, 3).\\n\\n| Major Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated 7-Day Total (JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-Range) | 15,000 - 25,000 | 105,000 - 175,000 | *Ryokan* stay recommended for 1-2 nights. |\\n| **Food & Dining** | 5,000 - 10,000 | 35,000 - 70,000 | Includes street food, casual lunches, and 1-2 higher-end dinners. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | 1,500 - 2,500 | 10,500 - 17,500 | Average temple entry is 500-600 JPY. |\\n| **Local Transport** | 1,000 - 1,500 | 7,000 - 10,500 | IC card use + occasional day passes. |\\n| **Cultural Activities** (Workshops/Tea) | Variable (Budget 5,000 JPY per activity) | ~15,000 | Allocated for 2 major workshops/ceremonies. |\\n| **Estimated Total (Excluding Flights/Insurance)** | **23,000 - 39,000+** | **Approx. 172,500 - 283,000 JPY** | *(USD conversion highly recommended for budgeting)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying near the historical centers offers convenience and atmosphere:\\n\\n1.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, proximity to traditional streets, and evening walks. Excellent access to many major eastern temples.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Best for modern convenience, dining variety, and easy subway/bus connectivity to all directions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Gion Immersion (Tradition Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Dining Recommendation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Arrival & Check-in.** Activate ICOCA card. | 2 hours | **Lunch:** Casual **Udon/Soba** near your accommodation. |\\n| **3:00 PM** | **Tea Ceremony Experience.** Book a private or small-group ceremony in the Gion or Higashiyama area to begin your cultural immersion immediately. (Pre-book essential!) | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Explore Hanamikoji Street (Gion).** Walk the historic streets, observing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. | 1 hour | |\\n| **6:30 PM** | **Dinner & Geiko Spotting.** Dine early. Walk cautiously through Gion after dark, respecting the privacy of *Geiko* and *Maiko*. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Try **Kyoto-style *Obanzai*** (home-style cooking) in a small Gion eatery. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to accommodation. | - | |\\n\\n**Cultural Note:** Pay close attention to the etiquette learned in the tea ceremony (removing shoes, sitting posture).\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty (Iconic Temples & Preserved Streets)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:30 AM** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple.** Arrive *before* 8:00 AM to enjoy the wooden stage with minimal crowds. | 2 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:00 AM** | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka.** Wander down these iconic, preserved sloping streets. Browse traditional craft shops. | 1.5 hours | **Snack:** Try *Yatsuhashi* (cinnamon sweet). |\\n| **11:30 AM** | **Kodaiji Temple.** A beautiful Zen temple nearby, often quieter than Kiyomizu-dera. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Higashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Vegetarian *Sh\u014djin Ry\u014dri* (Zen Buddhist Cuisine) if available nearby, or a light cafe lunch. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Yasaka Shrine & Maruyama Park.** Explore the main shrine and relax in the adjacent park. | 1.5 hours | Walkable from the previous stop. |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Optional: Visit Chion-in Temple** (Impressive main gate). | 1 hour | |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner & Relaxation.** | | **Dinner:** Enjoy **Ramen** in the Kawaramachi area for a hearty meal. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Grandeur and Zen Rock Gardens (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:30 AM** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion).** Aim for opening time to see the best reflections before peak crowds arrive. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Ryoan-ji Temple.** Famous for its abstract, enigmatic rock garden\u2014a pinnacle of Zen aesthetics. | 1.5 hours | Short bus ride or 25 min walk from Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Temples.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Simple set meal (*teishoku*) at a local eatery. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Ninna-ji Temple (If time allows).** A large complex with beautiful pagodas and temple grounds. | 2 hours | |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Return to City Center.** Browse for high-quality crafts or traditional items. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **Evening** | **Special Dinner Experience.** | | **Dinner:** Splurge on **Kyoto Beef** or enjoy high-quality **Sushi**. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates and Sacred Statues (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:00 AM** | **Fushimi Inari Shrine.** Start *very early* (before 7:30 AM) to experience the magic of the *torii* gates without the crush of tourists. Hike as far as desired. | 3 - 4 hours | **Transport:** JR Nara Line (ICOCA Card) to Inari Station. |\\n| **11:00 AM** | **Return & Travel North.** Head toward Higashiyama/Central area. | 1 hour | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near Sanjusangendo.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Quick, local stop near the temple. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Sanjusangendo Temple.** Marvel at the 1,001 statues of Kannon. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access is best from Kyoto Station area. |\\n| **4:00 PM** | **Kyoto National Museum (Optional).** Deepen your understanding of Japanese art and history. | 2 hours | Located close to Sanjusangendo. |\\n| **Evening** | **Relaxed Dinner.** | | **Dinner:** Explore **Pontocho Alley** for atmosphere and dining options along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature and Bamboo Serenity (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:00 AM** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** Head here immediately upon arrival in the area to walk through the grove before the major crowds arrive. | 1 hour | **Transport:** JR Sagano Line (ICOCA) to Saga-Arashiyama Station. |\\n| **9:30 AM** | **Tenryu-ji Temple.** Explore the stunning Zen garden, which transitions beautifully into the surrounding natural landscape. | 2 hours | Adjacent to the Bamboo Grove exit. |\\n| **12:00 PM** | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk.** Enjoy views of the Katsura River. | 1 hour | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Arashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Okochi Sanso Villa.** The former estate of a famous actor. Offers incredible panoramic views and a quieter experience than the main grove. Includes tea upon exit. | 2 hours | A slight climb, but highly rewarding. |\\n| **Evening** | **Return to Central Kyoto.** | | **Dinner:** Casual dinner near your hotel or try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n\\n### Day 6: Philosophers' Path and Cultural Workshop (Northeast Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion).** Appreciate its dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku No Michi).** Walk south along the picturesque canal, stopping to admire smaller shrines. | 1.5 - 2 hours | Perfect for quiet reflection. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Path.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Cafe lunch or quick bite near the northern end of the path. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Specialized Cultural Workshop.** Book a workshop for deeper engagement (e.g., *Ikebana* flower arranging, calligraphy, or *Wagashi* sweet making). | 2.5 hours | Confirm location beforehand; often near central or eastern Kyoto. |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Optional: Nanzen-ji Temple.** Explore the grounds and the striking aqueduct near the entrance. | 1.5 hours | Beautiful spot as the sun starts to set. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner Planning.** | | **Dinner:** Final meal emphasizing your favorite Japanese cuisine from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Quiet Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Honen-in Temple OR Daitoku-ji Complex.** Choose a less crowded, more reflective temple site to conclude your temple visits. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access for both locations. |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Souvenir Shopping.** Pick up last-minute quality gifts (high-grade green tea, incense, crafts). Nishiki Market is an option, but can be crowded. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Final Lunch.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** A final, favorite Japanese meal or something quick near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Departure.** Travel to Kyoto Station for Shinkansen or airport access. | As required | Allow ample time for airport transfers. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Etiquette Reminder:** Remember to practice respectful silence in temples, thank staff with *Okini*, and never photograph *Geiko* without permission. Enjoy your profound cultural journey!\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-f2ef503f-ae2d-4fe2-b8d4-40c7327c0051\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"As your TravelOptimizerAgent, I have integrated crucial money-saving strategies, specific packing lists, contingency plans, and essential local resources into your detailed 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary.\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary: Kyoto (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary is designed for a comprehensive cultural immersion in Kyoto, balancing iconic historical sites with intimate experiences of Zen, tea traditions, and natural beauty.\\n\\n**Best Time to Travel:** Spring (Cherry Blossoms) or Autumn (Fall Foliage).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Area\\n\\nFor a first-time deep dive focusing on culture and accessibility, consider staying in or near:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama (East Kyoto):** Excellent access to Kiyomizu-dera, Gion, and the Philosopher's Path. Very atmospheric.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi Stations):** Best for transportation links (subway and buses) and modern dining/shopping convenience.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Day-by-Day Schedule (With Optimizations)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & The Iconic East (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Arrival at KIX/ITM, Transfer to Kyoto, Check-in | **BUDGET TIP:** If using KIX, the *Haruka Express* is fast but expensive. Consider the cheaper **JR Local Rapid Service** followed by a transfer, or the **Airport Limousine Bus** for direct, often cheaper service to Kyoto Station. |\\n| Lunch | Simple Udon or Soba near your hotel. | **MONEY SAVER:** Avoid restaurants directly facing major tourist spots (like the street leading to Kiyomizu-dera). Walk 2-3 blocks away for better value. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | **CULTURAL DO\u2019s/DON\u2019Ts:** Use the purification fountain (Temizuya) correctly: ladle water, wash left hand, then right, pour water into your cupped left hand to rinse the ladle handle, then discard water. |\\n| Late Afternoon | Stroll **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets | **PACKING:** Wear comfortable, slip-on shoes. You will encounter many steep, uneven stone steps and some shops/temples require shoe removal. |\\n| Dinner | **Higashiyama Dinner** | **BACKUP:** If Pontocho/Gion is booked, try the dining options inside the Shijo Kawaramachi department stores (like Takashimaya or Daimaru) for high-quality but cheaper set menus at lunchtime or early dinner. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Zen, Tea, and Geiko Culture\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience** | **MONEY SAVER:** Book a group ceremony rather than a private one. Look for ceremonies held in public community centers or smaller temples rather than high-end tourist venues for savings of 30-50%. |\\n| Lunch | Lunch in Gion | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Tabelog** (Japan's primary restaurant review site) to find highly-rated, lower-priced lunch sets (*teishoku*) in the Gion area. |\\n| Afternoon | **Kodai-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not use flash photography anywhere inside temple buildings, even if it's not explicitly forbidden, as it degrades the art and disturbs visitors. |\\n| Evening | **Gion District Walk** | **CRUCIAL DON'T:** Never attempt to stop, touch, or photograph a Geiko/Maiko if they are clearly rushing. Respect their work commute. Stay on public sidewalks. |\\n| Dinner | **Pontocho Alley Dining** | **BACKUP PLAN:** If Pontocho is too busy or expensive, explore the smaller side alleys branching off Shijo Dori for less crowded *yakitori* joints. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Splendor and Contemplation\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | **MONEY SAVER:** Entry fees are fixed, but arriving right at opening (9 AM) avoids paying for entry when the sun is harsh and crowds are thick, maximizing the visual experience for the standard fee. |\\n| Transit | Travel to Ryoan-ji | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Purchase an **ICOCA/Suica Card** immediately. This refillable card streamlines bus/subway travel immensely\u2014no need to fumble for exact change or bus passes daily. |\\n| Afternoon | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit quietly for at least 10 minutes at the viewing platform. The garden is designed for quiet contemplation; rushing it defeats the purpose of this Zen stop. |\\n| Late Afternoon | **Nishijin Textile Center (Optional)** | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If skipping the center, visit a small, independent craft shop in the Nishijin area to see artisans at work without the tourist shop markup. |\\n| Dinner | **Central Kyoto/Nishiki Market Area** | **MONEY SAVER:** Nishiki Market vendors often sell ready-to-eat foods (pickles, mochi, skewers). Grab a selection of these for a delicious, varied, and cheap dinner feast. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates of Inari\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | **BACKUP PLAN (Heat/Rain):** If hiking in extreme heat or heavy rain, only go up to the first major junction (Yotsutsuji intersection) for the famous view, then descend. The upper mountain paths can be exposed. |\\n| Lunch | **Inari Street Food** | **CULTURAL DO:** Trying *Kitsune Udon* (udon noodles with sweet fried tofu) is highly recommended here, as the fox (*kitsune*) is the messenger of the Inari deity. |\\n| Afternoon | Return to Central Kyoto & Rest | **PACKING:** Bring moisture-wicking socks and a small towel. You will sweat while hiking Inari, and local washrooms/sento often charge for towel rental. |\\n| Evening | **Local Izakaya Experience** | **CULTURAL DO:** When ordering at an Izakaya, expect a small cover charge called *ot\u014dshi* or *tsukidashi*\u2014this is mandatory and includes a small appetizer. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature Immersion\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | Travel to Arashiyama & **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple** | **MONEY SAVER:** Tenry\u016b-ji has two entry fees: the main hall/garden and the separate Bamboo Grove access. If you are on a tight budget, skip the main hall but walk through the famous **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** (Day 5 PM activity) which is free to access from the back paths. |\\n| Lunch | Riverside Dining | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy high-quality *bento* boxes from a department store basement (*depachika*) near Kyoto Station before heading out, and have a scenic picnic lunch by the river. |\\n| Afternoon | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove** | **LOCAL RESOURCE:** Download an offline map of Arashiyama. The paths diverge, and good signage is sometimes lacking deeper in the woods. |\\n| Evening | **Relaxation / Onsen** | **PACKING/CULTURAL NOTE:** If visiting a public bath/onsen, you must be comfortable bathing nude (separately for men/women). No tattoos are allowed in many traditional establishments\u2014research tattoo-friendly options like **Tendon no Yu** if this is a concern. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Shogun Power and Secluded Zen\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Nijo-jo Castle** | **CULTURAL DON'T:** Do not step on the edges of the \\\"nightingale floors\\\" as they are more likely to squeak when stepped on near the perimeter. Walk carefully in the center if possible. |\\n| Afternoon | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex** | **ZEN TIP:** Select *one* sub-temple (e.g., Koto-in) to focus on deeply, as paying entry fees for too many small temples drains the budget quickly. The larger Daitoku-ji grounds are free to wander. |\\n| Evening | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku-no-michi) Walk** | **BACKUP PLAN (Rain):** If it is raining heavily, substitute this walk with a visit to the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design** near Miyako Messe for an indoor cultural experience. |\\n| Dinner | **Near Demachiyanagi or Imadegawa** | **MONEY SAVER:** This university-adjacent area (near Kyoto University) has many excellent, cheap, and authentic restaurants catering to students. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Masterpieces & Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Optimization Notes & Budget Tips |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| Morning | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple** | **CULTURAL DO:** Photography is strictly forbidden inside. Before entering, pause outside to meditate on the sheer scale of the statues; this helps prepare your mind for the overwhelming experience inside. |\\n| Midday | **Final Reflection Spot (Honen-in Temple)** | **PACKING:** Pick up high-quality, long-lasting souvenirs like Kyoto incense (*Oko*) or preserved tea leaves today rather than waiting until the station. |\\n| Lunch | **Kyoto Station Area** | **BUDGET TIP:** Head to the Isetan department store food halls (*depachika*) around 1 PM. Some high-end deli/bakery items are deeply discounted near closing time (usually 6 PM onwards, check local schedules). |\\n| Afternoon | Souvenir Shopping & Departure Prep | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Google Maps** for real-time navigation to the airport transfer point, confirming the final platform/gate number for the Haruka Express or Shinkansen. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Optimization Deep Dive\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcb0 Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **Transportation Flexibility:** Rely primarily on walking and the efficient bus network. Purchase the **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass (700 JPY)** only if you plan 3 or more bus rides. For days using the JR lines (Inari, Arashiyama), an ICOCA/Suica card is best.\\n2.  **Temple Passes:** Most major temples charge between 400\u2013600 JPY entry. If you find yourself consistently skipping afternoon visits due to fatigue or budget strain, prioritize only the UNESCO sites (Kiyomizu, Kinkaku-ji, Tenryu-ji) to save entry fees.\\n3.  **Dining Strategy:** Embrace *Konbini* (convenience stores like 7-Eleven, Lawson, Family Mart) for excellent, fresh breakfasts (Onigiri, Sandwiches) for under 400 JPY. For dinner, prioritize lunch sets (*teishoku*) which offer the same quality as dinner but at half the price.\\n4.  **Water:** Carry a reusable water bottle. Vending machines are everywhere (and cheaper than buying bottled water in stores), and many temples have public water sources for refilling (check signage).\\n\\n### \ud83e\uddf3 Packing Recommendations (Kyoto Specific)\\n\\n*   **Comfortable Footwear:** Non-negotiable. You will easily walk 8-10 miles daily over uneven paving, gravel, and stairs.\\n*   **Layered Clothing:** Evenings can be cool, especially near the rivers or when entering shady temple halls, even if the day is warm. Pack light, breathable layers.\\n*   **Small, Foldable Umbrella/Rain Shell:** Essential, especially during the rainy season (June) or unpredictable shoulder seasons.\\n*   **Small Gift (Omiyage):** If visiting a private tea ceremony or seeking specific artisanal services, having a small, attractively packaged souvenir from your home country (or a standard Japanese gift item) is a very polite gesture.\\n*   **Portable Charger:** Early mornings and late evenings mean relying heavily on navigation apps; keep your phone charged.\\n\\n### \ud83c\udf27\ufe0f Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Backup Activity / Contingency Plan |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon Warning** | **Day 5/6 Substitute:** Visit the **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (Central Kyoto), explore the massive underground shopping malls around Kyoto Station, or spend a long afternoon in the **Kyoto National Museum** or the **Kyoto Museum of Crafts and Design**. |\\n| **Extreme Heat (Summer)** | Focus on sites with excellent air conditioning (Nijo-jo, Museums). Schedule Fushimi Inari for pre-sunrise (5:30 AM start) and retreat indoors between 11 AM and 3 PM. Utilize public transport heavily. |\\n| **Unexpected Temple Closure** | Many small temples close for maintenance or private ceremonies. **Backup Plan:** Head to the nearby **Shinto Shrines** (which often have different opening hours) or explore local, unnamed neighborhood temples\u2014these are often the most authentic finds. |\\n| **Transportation Strikes/Delays** | If the JR line to Arashiyama is delayed, use the **Randen Tram** instead. It\u2019s slower but offers a lovely, scenic ride through older districts. |\\n\\n### \ud83d\udcf1 Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for bus routes, walking times, and train schedules. Turn on \\\"Public Transit\\\" layers.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) or Navitime:** Excellent secondary resources for complex train routes involving multiple carriers (e.g., Shinkansen connections).\\n3.  **Tabelog:** For reliable restaurant reviews and finding local gems away from tourist traps.\\n4.  **Google Translate:** Download the Japanese language pack for offline translation, particularly useful for reading menus or signs.\\n5.  **ICOCA/Suica App:** If you use a digital wallet on your phone, you can load and manage your transport card digitally, eliminating the need for a physical card.\\n\\n### \u26e9\ufe0f Cultural Do's & Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| Do | Don't |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** use quiet, respectful tones inside temples, shrines, and on public transport. | **DON'T** eat, drink, or smoke while walking down the historic streets (Sannenzaka, Gion). Find a designated spot. |\\n| **DO** learn a few key phrases: *Arigatou gozaimasu* (Thank you very much) and *Sumimasen* (Excuse me/Sorry). | **DON'T** take pictures of people without asking, especially Geiko/Maiko or people performing religious rites. |\\n| **DO** accept business cards (if exchanged) with both hands and examine them briefly before putting them away respectfully. | **DON'T** leave trash behind. Carry a small plastic bag for wrappers and bottles until you find a trash can. |\\n| **DO** remove your shoes promptly when entering homes, certain temples, and traditional restaurants/tea houses. | **DON'T** point with your index finger; use an open hand gesture instead. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640594.995979,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.59251968503937,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___9106fa87-7f9a-4061-9cf1-3ed652509f89\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___9106fa87-7f9a-4061-9cf1-3ed652509f89\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion in Kyoto, blending iconic temples, traditional arts, and natural serenity, structured logically for efficient travel using Kyoto's bus and train network.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Pre-Trip Essentials & Budget Estimates\\n\\n**Best Travel Season Recommendation:** Late October or Mid-November (Autumn Foliage).\\n**Transportation Tip:** Purchase an **ICOCA Card** upon arrival for seamless travel on buses and trains. Budget for a **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass** (approx. 700 JPY) on heavy bus travel days (Days 2, 3).\\n\\n| Major Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated 7-Day Total (JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-Range) | 15,000 - 25,000 | 105,000 - 175,000 | *Ryokan* stay recommended for 1-2 nights. |\\n| **Food & Dining** | 5,000 - 10,000 | 35,000 - 70,000 | Includes street food, casual lunches, and 1-2 higher-end dinners. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | 1,500 - 2,500 | 10,500 - 17,500 | Average temple entry is 500-600 JPY. |\\n| **Local Transport** | 1,000 - 1,500 | 7,000 - 10,500 | IC card use + occasional day passes. |\\n| **Cultural Activities** (Workshops/Tea) | Variable (Budget 5,000 JPY per activity) | ~15,000 | Allocated for 2 major workshops/ceremonies. |\\n| **Estimated Total (Excluding Flights/Insurance)** | **23,000 - 39,000+** | **Approx. 172,500 - 283,000 JPY** | *(USD conversion highly recommended for budgeting)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying near the historical centers offers convenience and atmosphere:\\n\\n1.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, proximity to traditional streets, and evening walks. Excellent access to many major eastern temples.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Best for modern convenience, dining variety, and easy subway/bus connectivity to all directions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Gion Immersion (Tradition Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Dining Recommendation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Arrival & Check-in.** Activate ICOCA card. | 2 hours | **Lunch:** Casual **Udon/Soba** near your accommodation. |\\n| **3:00 PM** | **Tea Ceremony Experience.** Book a private or small-group ceremony in the Gion or Higashiyama area to begin your cultural immersion immediately. (Pre-book essential!) | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Explore Hanamikoji Street (Gion).** Walk the historic streets, observing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. | 1 hour | |\\n| **6:30 PM** | **Dinner & Geiko Spotting.** Dine early. Walk cautiously through Gion after dark, respecting the privacy of *Geiko* and *Maiko*. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Try **Kyoto-style *Obanzai*** (home-style cooking) in a small Gion eatery. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to accommodation. | - | |\\n\\n**Cultural Note:** Pay close attention to the etiquette learned in the tea ceremony (removing shoes, sitting posture).\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty (Iconic Temples & Preserved Streets)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:30 AM** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple.** Arrive *before* 8:00 AM to enjoy the wooden stage with minimal crowds. | 2 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:00 AM** | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka.** Wander down these iconic, preserved sloping streets. Browse traditional craft shops. | 1.5 hours | **Snack:** Try *Yatsuhashi* (cinnamon sweet). |\\n| **11:30 AM** | **Kodaiji Temple.** A beautiful Zen temple nearby, often quieter than Kiyomizu-dera. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Higashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Vegetarian *Sh\u014djin Ry\u014dri* (Zen Buddhist Cuisine) if available nearby, or a light cafe lunch. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Yasaka Shrine & Maruyama Park.** Explore the main shrine and relax in the adjacent park. | 1.5 hours | Walkable from the previous stop. |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Optional: Visit Chion-in Temple** (Impressive main gate). | 1 hour | |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner & Relaxation.** | | **Dinner:** Enjoy **Ramen** in the Kawaramachi area for a hearty meal. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Grandeur and Zen Rock Gardens (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:30 AM** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion).** Aim for opening time to see the best reflections before peak crowds arrive. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Ryoan-ji Temple.** Famous for its abstract, enigmatic rock garden\u2014a pinnacle of Zen aesthetics. | 1.5 hours | Short bus ride or 25 min walk from Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Temples.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Simple set meal (*teishoku*) at a local eatery. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Ninna-ji Temple (If time allows).** A large complex with beautiful pagodas and temple grounds. | 2 hours | |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Return to City Center.** Browse for high-quality crafts or traditional items. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **Evening** | **Special Dinner Experience.** | | **Dinner:** Splurge on **Kyoto Beef** or enjoy high-quality **Sushi**. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates and Sacred Statues (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:00 AM** | **Fushimi Inari Shrine.** Start *very early* (before 7:30 AM) to experience the magic of the *torii* gates without the crush of tourists. Hike as far as desired. | 3 - 4 hours | **Transport:** JR Nara Line (ICOCA Card) to Inari Station. |\\n| **11:00 AM** | **Return & Travel North.** Head toward Higashiyama/Central area. | 1 hour | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near Sanjusangendo.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Quick, local stop near the temple. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Sanjusangendo Temple.** Marvel at the 1,001 statues of Kannon. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access is best from Kyoto Station area. |\\n| **4:00 PM** | **Kyoto National Museum (Optional).** Deepen your understanding of Japanese art and history. | 2 hours | Located close to Sanjusangendo. |\\n| **Evening** | **Relaxed Dinner.** | | **Dinner:** Explore **Pontocho Alley** for atmosphere and dining options along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature and Bamboo Serenity (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:00 AM** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** Head here immediately upon arrival in the area to walk through the grove before the major crowds arrive. | 1 hour | **Transport:** JR Sagano Line (ICOCA) to Saga-Arashiyama Station. |\\n| **9:30 AM** | **Tenryu-ji Temple.** Explore the stunning Zen garden, which transitions beautifully into the surrounding natural landscape. | 2 hours | Adjacent to the Bamboo Grove exit. |\\n| **12:00 PM** | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk.** Enjoy views of the Katsura River. | 1 hour | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Arashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Okochi Sanso Villa.** The former estate of a famous actor. Offers incredible panoramic views and a quieter experience than the main grove. Includes tea upon exit. | 2 hours | A slight climb, but highly rewarding. |\\n| **Evening** | **Return to Central Kyoto.** | | **Dinner:** Casual dinner near your hotel or try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n\\n### Day 6: Philosophers' Path and Cultural Workshop (Northeast Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion).** Appreciate its dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku No Michi).** Walk south along the picturesque canal, stopping to admire smaller shrines. | 1.5 - 2 hours | Perfect for quiet reflection. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Path.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Cafe lunch or quick bite near the northern end of the path. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Specialized Cultural Workshop.** Book a workshop for deeper engagement (e.g., *Ikebana* flower arranging, calligraphy, or *Wagashi* sweet making). | 2.5 hours | Confirm location beforehand; often near central or eastern Kyoto. |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Optional: Nanzen-ji Temple.** Explore the grounds and the striking aqueduct near the entrance. | 1.5 hours | Beautiful spot as the sun starts to set. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner Planning.** | | **Dinner:** Final meal emphasizing your favorite Japanese cuisine from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Quiet Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Honen-in Temple OR Daitoku-ji Complex.** Choose a less crowded, more reflective temple site to conclude your temple visits. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access for both locations. |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Souvenir Shopping.** Pick up last-minute quality gifts (high-grade green tea, incense, crafts). Nishiki Market is an option, but can be crowded. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Final Lunch.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** A final, favorite Japanese meal or something quick near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Departure.** Travel to Kyoto Station for Shinkansen or airport access. | As required | Allow ample time for airport transfers. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Etiquette Reminder:** Remember to practice respectful silence in temples, thank staff with *Okini*, and never photograph *Geiko* without permission. Enjoy your profound cultural journey!\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"cc1e7b75-64d7-4050-b600-9fc94a213dc7\",\"destination_research\":\"It sounds like you are planning a wonderful and rich experience! Kyoto is the perfect destination for a cultural deep-dive focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\nHere is a comprehensive plan covering the essential aspects of your trip research.\\n\\n## Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive: 7-Day Itinerary Research\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are during **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Pleasant and comfortable weather, ideal for viewing cherry blossoms (Sakura), which typically bloom from late March to early April. May also has mild weather with average highs around 20\u00b0C (68\u00b0F). Be aware that late April/early May includes **Golden Week**, a period of intense domestic travel with large crowds and higher prices.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Features cool, comfortable weather and stunning fall foliage, often peaking in November. October has sunny and warm days with cool evenings. November has average temperatures around 13\u00b0C (55\u00b0F).\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, with temperatures dropping to around 5\u00b0C (41\u00b0F) in January, but offering a serene atmosphere with occasional snow.\\n\\n**Recommendation for Cultural Deep-Dive:** **Late October or mid-November** would be ideal to combine comfortable walking weather with the natural beauty of autumn, enhancing your nature and temple exploration.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions and Must-See Locations (Temple & Nature Focus)\\n\\nKyoto is renowned for its historical sites, boasting over 2,000 religious structures.\\n\\n#### Iconic Temples & Shrines:\\n*   **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** One of the most iconic and frequently visited temples, famous for its reflection in the pond. Visit early to avoid crowds.\\n*   **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** A UNESCO World Heritage Site with a famous wooden veranda overlooking the city. It is very popular and often crowded.\\n*   **Fushimi Inari Shrine:** Famous for its thousands of bright orange *torii* gates that line a mountain trail. Great for a morning walk.\\n*   **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Known for its unique sand garden and classic architecture.\\n*   **Tenryu-ji Temple:** Located in Arashiyama, it is a major Zen temple complex featuring one of Kyoto's finest landscape gardens.\\n*   **Sanjusangendo (Rengeoin):** Remarkable for its 120m-long hall housing 1,001 life-sized statues of Kannon.\\n\\n#### Nature & Tradition Integration:\\n*   **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** A must-see natural spot, easily paired with a visit to **Tenryu-ji Temple**.\\n*   **Nanzen-ji Temple:** Situated at the base of the Higashiyama mountains, it is a beautiful Zen temple, especially scenic during cherry blossom or autumn foliage seasons.\\n*   **Honen-in Temple:** A quieter, less-visited temple known for its lush gardens and moss-covered gate, perfect for quiet reflection.\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition, so understanding etiquette is crucial for a deep cultural experience.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Show Respect:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Temizuya (Purification Fountain):** If you use the purification fountain, follow the ritual: cleanse your left hand, then your right hand, rinse your mouth (do not touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tea rooms; slip-on shoes are convenient. Wear clean socks.\\n    *   **Photography:** Do not take photos inside buildings or where signs prohibit it. Avoid using flash and do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Noise Level:** Maintain a quiet demeanor; temples are places for prayer and worship.\\n    *   **Food/Drink:** Do not eat or drink in sacred areas.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan; a polite \\\"Okini\\\" (thank you in the Kansai dialect) is sufficient gratitude in Kyoto.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up orderly when waiting for buses, trains, or stores.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not bother or stop apprentice geisha (*maiko*) or *geiko* for photos; they are often on their way to appointments.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has a dense public transport network, but it's less interconnected than in some other major cities.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Considered the **best way to get around** for reaching most major attractions like Kinkaku-ji and Kiyomizu-dera. You typically enter at the back, push a button to request a stop, and pay a fixed fare (around 230 yen) at the front when exiting.\\n*   **Trains & Subways:** Two subway lines (Karasuma North-South, Tozai East-West) connect major hubs, but few temples are near subway stations. Trains (JR and private lines like Keihan, Hankyu) are excellent for day trips (e.g., Fushimi Inari, Nara, Arashiyama).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Use an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, PASMO) as they are valid on virtually all buses and trains in Kyoto and across Japan.\\n*   **Walking & Cycling:** Kyoto is relatively flat and excellent for walking, especially in districts like Gion and Higashiyama. Cycling is also a highly recommended alternative.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and useful, but an added cost. Rear doors open and close automatically\u2014do not touch them.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Exercise common sense precautions as you would at home.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:** Dial **119** for ambulance or fire services; dial **110** for police.\\n*   **Wildlife:** Be aware of occasional sightings of Asiatic black bears in mountainous areas; travel in groups and carry noise-making items if hiking.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:**\\n    *   **Visa:** Check with the nearest Japanese embassy/consulate for current visa exemption rules for your nationality (e.g., US citizens are often eligible for visa-free short stays up to 90 days).\\n    *   **Insurance:** It is highly advisable to take out travel insurance to cover medical costs.\\n    *   **Overtourism:** Be prepared for large crowds and long lines at major sites, as tourism is high.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Suggested 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Focus\\n\\nBased on your preferences, here is a thematic breakdown for your 7 days:\\n\\n| Day Focus | Suggested Areas/Activities | Cultural/Nature Link |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Gion Exploration** | Check into accommodation (perhaps a *Ryokan*), walk **Hanamikoji Street** in Gion, and participate in your first **Tea Ceremony** experience in the area. |\\n| **Day 2** | **Eastern Kyoto - Iconic Temples** | Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** (early!), explore the charming, preserved streets of **Higashiyama** (Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka), and perhaps visit **Kodaiji Temple** or **Yasaka Shrine**. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Golden & Zen North** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion). Afternoon dedicated to **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Ninna-ji Temple**. |\\n| **Day 4** | **Southern Path - Torii & Statues** | Morning hike through **Fushimi Inari Shrine's** thousands of *torii* gates. Afternoon visit to **Sanjusangendo** to see the 1001 statues. |\\n| **Day 5** | **Arashiyama Nature & Zen** | Day trip to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenryu-ji Temple** and its garden, walk through the **Bamboo Grove**, and consider visiting the quieter **Okochi Sanso Villa** for views. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Tradition & Tranquility** | Morning at **Ginkaku-ji** (Silver Pavilion) followed by a walk along the **Philosopher's Path** (great for nature/reflection). Afternoon dedicated to a specialized cultural workshop (e.g., calligraphy or *ikebana* flower arranging). |\\n| **Day 7** | **Hidden Gems & Departure** | Visit a quieter temple like **Honen-in** or explore **Daitoku-ji** complex. Conclude with any final souvenir shopping before departure. |\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null