YZp5gGyPqucdH0yl1etAfmWuWN97NbXJOn7g5H2dnyF_P2kYtlOfyOG9x4oKt9HQ==\\\">Kyoto cultural etiquette and customs</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGGudbWkCza0nZfvzjVpdtkKRK_vqABSoDx68CT8TIMpVB_IiuEoyj79iTwDUioBqLYWyWzmvS7dEhdBGwu0YTAsid3eIHvxnuy5NGBLyHDx_Ua9Tl2IGZdeW0iaVKT_hHIO7Mt4APomrtqBUtInxScDB8hSsdtr9doof-FRTuu5GW6f6rsMMJ_2_2VtgSeByVuxY7y18OYTpkV7qZCEF_A2bE5\\\">best time to visit Kyoto weather</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHvon6nDUANTCS2Z1-Up7-wpc0WSFVitwt0bWADpH81ky-ACFyoGl-sL3gVl4sX-aCKNhozzkLl4IyZx84_9Prooq29NJ5LofpJbEUOLGgyI3_uYRexycX9OeQis6Ixhxll8R8RowBREj5GmxwGQlCI_57DBPi9R4Q2756cTfienH9ZbsljRZxgGNAlneoWT7iHssUbSdnxl-uUlCH1aRAzyck6xA==\\\">Kyoto top temples and attractions</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQELUrstcQhzrq38HzmDl5l5pWG7OyNkm14w_SF0JaADRrzCOVXV-QMM8Ariyg_e-H4AiLgZlZUwHaFjXEZyyFCvLcBDT8q0ZDOdlznuI5GAcm8wfoE8q_2eV7L2AF2084jN22RIhud0SXxxMiTrtrzqVaQ9R0gHZz98CXfEt0h26K_9Wc8qRhAoLnUQytkBNT7kbtOwxfz4PMm07Rd7ezuVQEU1Eb2_Bg==\\\">Kyoto safety and travel requirements</a>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\",\"sdk_blob\":null},\"source_flagging_uris\":null,\"web_search_queries\":[\"best time to visit Kyoto weather\",\"Kyoto top temples and attractions\",\"Kyoto cultural etiquette and customs\",\"transportation in Kyoto for tourists\",\"Kyoto safety and travel requirements\"]},\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"4f7984c3-df41-4e27-bee9-51b830242f8a\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":2066,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":142,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"thoughts_token_count\":null,\"tool_use_prompt_token_count\":142,\"tool_use_prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"total_token_count\":2350,\"traffic_type\":null},\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-92e9106e-165a-4126-94ef-6c48c0bff47e\",\"author\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"4f7984c3-df41-4e27-bee9-51b830242f8a\",\"destination_research\":\"It sounds like you are planning a wonderful and rich experience! Kyoto is the perfect destination for a cultural deep-dive focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\nHere is a comprehensive plan covering the essential aspects of your trip research.\\n\\n## Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive: 7-Day Itinerary Research\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are during **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Pleasant and comfortable weather, ideal for viewing cherry blossoms (Sakura), which typically bloom from late March to early April. May also has mild weather with average highs around 20\u00b0C (68\u00b0F). Be aware that late April/early May includes **Golden Week**, a period of intense domestic travel with large crowds and higher prices.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Features cool, comfortable weather and stunning fall foliage, often peaking in November. October has sunny and warm days with cool evenings. November has average temperatures around 13\u00b0C (55\u00b0F).\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, with temperatures dropping to around 5\u00b0C (41\u00b0F) in January, but offering a serene atmosphere with occasional snow.\\n\\n**Recommendation for Cultural Deep-Dive:** **Late October or mid-November** would be ideal to combine comfortable walking weather with the natural beauty of autumn, enhancing your nature and temple exploration.\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions and Must-See Locations (Temple & Nature Focus)\\n\\nKyoto is renowned for its historical sites, boasting over 2,000 religious structures.\\n\\n#### Iconic Temples & Shrines:\\n*   **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion):** One of the most iconic and frequently visited temples, famous for its reflection in the pond. Visit early to avoid crowds.\\n*   **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** A UNESCO World Heritage Site with a famous wooden veranda overlooking the city. It is very popular and often crowded.\\n*   **Fushimi Inari Shrine:** Famous for its thousands of bright orange *torii* gates that line a mountain trail. Great for a morning walk.\\n*   **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Known for its unique sand garden and classic architecture.\\n*   **Tenryu-ji Temple:** Located in Arashiyama, it is a major Zen temple complex featuring one of Kyoto's finest landscape gardens.\\n*   **Sanjusangendo (Rengeoin):** Remarkable for its 120m-long hall housing 1,001 life-sized statues of Kannon.\\n\\n#### Nature & Tradition Integration:\\n*   **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove:** A must-see natural spot, easily paired with a visit to **Tenryu-ji Temple**.\\n*   **Nanzen-ji Temple:** Situated at the base of the Higashiyama mountains, it is a beautiful Zen temple, especially scenic during cherry blossom or autumn foliage seasons.\\n*   **Honen-in Temple:** A quieter, less-visited temple known for its lush gardens and moss-covered gate, perfect for quiet reflection.\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values tradition, so understanding etiquette is crucial for a deep cultural experience.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Show Respect:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n    *   **Temizuya (Purification Fountain):** If you use the purification fountain, follow the ritual: cleanse your left hand, then your right hand, rinse your mouth (do not touch the ladle to your lips), and finally rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Footwear:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering temple buildings or tea rooms; slip-on shoes are convenient. Wear clean socks.\\n    *   **Photography:** Do not take photos inside buildings or where signs prohibit it. Avoid using flash and do not photograph worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Noise Level:** Maintain a quiet demeanor; temples are places for prayer and worship.\\n    *   **Food/Drink:** Do not eat or drink in sacred areas.\\n*   **General Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is not customary in Japan; a polite \\\"Okini\\\" (thank you in the Kansai dialect) is sufficient gratitude in Kyoto.\\n    *   **Queuing:** Always line up orderly when waiting for buses, trains, or stores.\\n    *   **Maiko/Geiko:** Do not bother or stop apprentice geisha (*maiko*) or *geiko* for photos; they are often on their way to appointments.\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options\\n\\nKyoto has a dense public transport network, but it's less interconnected than in some other major cities.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Considered the **best way to get around** for reaching most major attractions like Kinkaku-ji and Kiyomizu-dera. You typically enter at the back, push a button to request a stop, and pay a fixed fare (around 230 yen) at the front when exiting.\\n*   **Trains & Subways:** Two subway lines (Karasuma North-South, Tozai East-West) connect major hubs, but few temples are near subway stations. Trains (JR and private lines like Keihan, Hankyu) are excellent for day trips (e.g., Fushimi Inari, Nara, Arashiyama).\\n*   **IC Cards:** Use an IC card (like ICOCA, Suica, PASMO) as they are valid on virtually all buses and trains in Kyoto and across Japan.\\n*   **Walking & Cycling:** Kyoto is relatively flat and excellent for walking, especially in districts like Gion and Higashiyama. Cycling is also a highly recommended alternative.\\n*   **Taxis:** Plentiful and useful, but an added cost. Rear doors open and close automatically\u2014do not touch them.\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Exercise common sense precautions as you would at home.\\n*   **Emergency Numbers:** Dial **119** for ambulance or fire services; dial **110** for police.\\n*   **Wildlife:** Be aware of occasional sightings of Asiatic black bears in mountainous areas; travel in groups and carry noise-making items if hiking.\\n*   **Travel Requirements:**\\n    *   **Visa:** Check with the nearest Japanese embassy/consulate for current visa exemption rules for your nationality (e.g., US citizens are often eligible for visa-free short stays up to 90 days).\\n    *   **Insurance:** It is highly advisable to take out travel insurance to cover medical costs.\\n    *   **Overtourism:** Be prepared for large crowds and long lines at major sites, as tourism is high.\\n\\n***\\n\\n### Suggested 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Focus\\n\\nBased on your preferences, here is a thematic breakdown for your 7 days:\\n\\n| Day Focus | Suggested Areas/Activities | Cultural/Nature Link |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Day 1** | **Arrival & Gion Exploration** | Check into accommodation (perhaps a *Ryokan*), walk **Hanamikoji Street** in Gion, and participate in your first **Tea Ceremony** experience in the area. |\\n| **Day 2** | **Eastern Kyoto - Iconic Temples** | Visit **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** (early!), explore the charming, preserved streets of **Higashiyama** (Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka), and perhaps visit **Kodaiji Temple** or **Yasaka Shrine**. |\\n| **Day 3** | **Golden & Zen North** | Morning visit to **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion). Afternoon dedicated to **Ryoan-ji** (famous rock garden) or **Ninna-ji Temple**. |\\n| **Day 4** | **Southern Path - Torii & Statues** | Morning hike through **Fushimi Inari Shrine's** thousands of *torii* gates. Afternoon visit to **Sanjusangendo** to see the 1001 statues. |\\n| **Day 5** | **Arashiyama Nature & Zen** | Day trip to **Arashiyama**. Visit **Tenryu-ji Temple** and its garden, walk through the **Bamboo Grove**, and consider visiting the quieter **Okochi Sanso Villa** for views. |\\n| **Day 6** | **Tradition & Tranquility** | Morning at **Ginkaku-ji** (Silver Pavilion) followed by a walk along the **Philosopher's Path** (great for nature/reflection). Afternoon dedicated to a specialized cultural workshop (e.g., calligraphy or *ikebana* flower arranging). |\\n| **Day 7** | **Hidden Gems & Departure** | Visit a quieter temple like **Honen-in** or explore **Daitoku-ji** complex. Conclude with any final souvenir shopping before departure. |\"},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"5e2fba85-c96f-493c-8f64-ed2afc3f2436\",\"timestamp\":1763640864.913521},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"# 7-Day Kyoto Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on maximizing your cultural immersion in Kyoto, blending iconic temples, traditional arts, and natural serenity, structured logically for efficient travel using Kyoto's bus and train network.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Pre-Trip Essentials & Budget Estimates\\n\\n**Best Travel Season Recommendation:** Late October or Mid-November (Autumn Foliage).\\n**Transportation Tip:** Purchase an **ICOCA Card** upon arrival for seamless travel on buses and trains. Budget for a **Kyoto City Bus One-Day Pass** (approx. 700 JPY) on heavy bus travel days (Days 2, 3).\\n\\n| Major Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated 7-Day Total (JPY) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation** (Mid-Range) | 15,000 - 25,000 | 105,000 - 175,000 | *Ryokan* stay recommended for 1-2 nights. |\\n| **Food & Dining** | 5,000 - 10,000 | 35,000 - 70,000 | Includes street food, casual lunches, and 1-2 higher-end dinners. |\\n| **Entrance Fees** | 1,500 - 2,500 | 10,500 - 17,500 | Average temple entry is 500-600 JPY. |\\n| **Local Transport** | 1,000 - 1,500 | 7,000 - 10,500 | IC card use + occasional day passes. |\\n| **Cultural Activities** (Workshops/Tea) | Variable (Budget 5,000 JPY per activity) | ~15,000 | Allocated for 2 major workshops/ceremonies. |\\n| **Estimated Total (Excluding Flights/Insurance)** | **23,000 - 39,000+** | **Approx. 172,500 - 283,000 JPY** | *(USD conversion highly recommended for budgeting)* |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, staying near the historical centers offers convenience and atmosphere:\\n\\n1.  **Gion/Higashiyama:** Best for atmosphere, proximity to traditional streets, and evening walks. Excellent access to many major eastern temples.\\n2.  **Central Kyoto (Near Karasuma/Kawaramachi):** Best for modern convenience, dining variety, and easy subway/bus connectivity to all directions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed Daily Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and Gion Immersion (Tradition Focus)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Dining Recommendation |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Arrival & Check-in.** Activate ICOCA card. | 2 hours | **Lunch:** Casual **Udon/Soba** near your accommodation. |\\n| **3:00 PM** | **Tea Ceremony Experience.** Book a private or small-group ceremony in the Gion or Higashiyama area to begin your cultural immersion immediately. (Pre-book essential!) | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Explore Hanamikoji Street (Gion).** Walk the historic streets, observing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. | 1 hour | |\\n| **6:30 PM** | **Dinner & Geiko Spotting.** Dine early. Walk cautiously through Gion after dark, respecting the privacy of *Geiko* and *Maiko*. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Try **Kyoto-style *Obanzai*** (home-style cooking) in a small Gion eatery. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to accommodation. | - | |\\n\\n**Cultural Note:** Pay close attention to the etiquette learned in the tea ceremony (removing shoes, sitting posture).\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty (Iconic Temples & Preserved Streets)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:30 AM** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple.** Arrive *before* 8:00 AM to enjoy the wooden stage with minimal crowds. | 2 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:00 AM** | **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka.** Wander down these iconic, preserved sloping streets. Browse traditional craft shops. | 1.5 hours | **Snack:** Try *Yatsuhashi* (cinnamon sweet). |\\n| **11:30 AM** | **Kodaiji Temple.** A beautiful Zen temple nearby, often quieter than Kiyomizu-dera. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Higashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Vegetarian *Sh\u014djin Ry\u014dri* (Zen Buddhist Cuisine) if available nearby, or a light cafe lunch. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Yasaka Shrine & Maruyama Park.** Explore the main shrine and relax in the adjacent park. | 1.5 hours | Walkable from the previous stop. |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Optional: Visit Chion-in Temple** (Impressive main gate). | 1 hour | |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner & Relaxation.** | | **Dinner:** Enjoy **Ramen** in the Kawaramachi area for a hearty meal. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Golden Grandeur and Zen Rock Gardens (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:30 AM** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion).** Aim for opening time to see the best reflections before peak crowds arrive. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Ryoan-ji Temple.** Famous for its abstract, enigmatic rock garden\u2014a pinnacle of Zen aesthetics. | 1.5 hours | Short bus ride or 25 min walk from Kinkaku-ji. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Temples.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Simple set meal (*teishoku*) at a local eatery. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Ninna-ji Temple (If time allows).** A large complex with beautiful pagodas and temple grounds. | 2 hours | |\\n| **4:30 PM** | **Return to City Center.** Browse for high-quality crafts or traditional items. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **Evening** | **Special Dinner Experience.** | | **Dinner:** Splurge on **Kyoto Beef** or enjoy high-quality **Sushi**. |\\n\\n### Day 4: The Thousand Gates and Sacred Statues (South Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **7:00 AM** | **Fushimi Inari Shrine.** Start *very early* (before 7:30 AM) to experience the magic of the *torii* gates without the crush of tourists. Hike as far as desired. | 3 - 4 hours | **Transport:** JR Nara Line (ICOCA Card) to Inari Station. |\\n| **11:00 AM** | **Return & Travel North.** Head toward Higashiyama/Central area. | 1 hour | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near Sanjusangendo.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Quick, local stop near the temple. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Sanjusangendo Temple.** Marvel at the 1,001 statues of Kannon. Photography is strictly prohibited inside. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access is best from Kyoto Station area. |\\n| **4:00 PM** | **Kyoto National Museum (Optional).** Deepen your understanding of Japanese art and history. | 2 hours | Located close to Sanjusangendo. |\\n| **Evening** | **Relaxed Dinner.** | | **Dinner:** Explore **Pontocho Alley** for atmosphere and dining options along the Kamo River (reservations recommended). |\\n\\n### Day 5: Arashiyama Nature and Bamboo Serenity (West Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **8:00 AM** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** Head here immediately upon arrival in the area to walk through the grove before the major crowds arrive. | 1 hour | **Transport:** JR Sagano Line (ICOCA) to Saga-Arashiyama Station. |\\n| **9:30 AM** | **Tenryu-ji Temple.** Explore the stunning Zen garden, which transitions beautifully into the surrounding natural landscape. | 2 hours | Adjacent to the Bamboo Grove exit. |\\n| **12:00 PM** | **Togetsukyo Bridge & River Walk.** Enjoy views of the Katsura River. | 1 hour | |\\n| **1:00 PM** | **Lunch in Arashiyama.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Try *Yudofu* (simmered tofu), an Arashiyama specialty. |\\n| **2:30 PM** | **Okochi Sanso Villa.** The former estate of a famous actor. Offers incredible panoramic views and a quieter experience than the main grove. Includes tea upon exit. | 2 hours | A slight climb, but highly rewarding. |\\n| **Evening** | **Return to Central Kyoto.** | | **Dinner:** Casual dinner near your hotel or try *Okonomiyaki* (savory pancake). |\\n\\n### Day 6: Philosophers' Path and Cultural Workshop (Northeast Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion).** Appreciate its dry sand garden (*Ginshadan*). | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus (Use One-Day Pass). |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Philosopher's Path (Tetsugaku No Michi).** Walk south along the picturesque canal, stopping to admire smaller shrines. | 1.5 - 2 hours | Perfect for quiet reflection. |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Lunch near the Path.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** Cafe lunch or quick bite near the northern end of the path. |\\n| **2:00 PM** | **Specialized Cultural Workshop.** Book a workshop for deeper engagement (e.g., *Ikebana* flower arranging, calligraphy, or *Wagashi* sweet making). | 2.5 hours | Confirm location beforehand; often near central or eastern Kyoto. |\\n| **5:00 PM** | **Optional: Nanzen-ji Temple.** Explore the grounds and the striking aqueduct near the entrance. | 1.5 hours | Beautiful spot as the sun starts to set. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner Planning.** | | **Dinner:** Final meal emphasizing your favorite Japanese cuisine from the week. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Quiet Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Notes/Transport |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **9:00 AM** | **Honen-in Temple OR Daitoku-ji Complex.** Choose a less crowded, more reflective temple site to conclude your temple visits. | 1.5 hours | **Transport:** Bus access for both locations. |\\n| **10:30 AM** | **Souvenir Shopping.** Pick up last-minute quality gifts (high-grade green tea, incense, crafts). Nishiki Market is an option, but can be crowded. | 1.5 hours | |\\n| **12:30 PM** | **Final Lunch.** | 1 hour | **Lunch:** A final, favorite Japanese meal or something quick near the station. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Departure.** Travel to Kyoto Station for Shinkansen or airport access. | As required | Allow ample time for airport transfers. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Etiquette Reminder:** Remember to practice respectful silence in temples, thank staff with *Okini*, and never photograph *Geiko* without permission. Enjoy your profound cultural journey!\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"84b34cdd-f65b-4ea8-a3ca-93dc45791b53\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":2803,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":2172,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":